A `break` and a `continue` survive a program that also lowers a context.

The context lowering rebuilds every statement list it walks, threading hidden
arguments onto the calls inside. A loop escape carries no expression and no
nested block, so its arm did nothing at all -- which in a walk that rebuilds
means the statement was deleted. Every target then emitted a loop body with
the escape gone and no diagnostic anywhere.

What made it survivable for so long is that it takes two features to reach:
the program needs an escape *and* something in the build has to declare a
context. `sngl:i18n`'s `#locale` is one, and so is the `listDepth` a list in
`sngl:ui/markup` reads -- so a package the program never imported could decide
whether its loops worked, which is why this fixture declares the context
itself rather than relying on one arriving from somewhere.
The window provides it, because a context nobody provides with a constant
default is folded away before the context lowering runs.

The assertion is the loop bodies in the goldens below: both escapes are in
them, under the `if` each was written in.
-- app.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"

output {
    go {
        bubbletea
    }
    none {
        html
    }
}

context #depth(0)

component main node {
    var xs list<int> = []
    var total = 0
    var seen = 0

    button(text="run", @click {
        for var x = xs {
            if x == 0 {
                continue
            }
            if x > 100 {
                break
            }
            total = total + x
        }
        seen = depth
    })
    text(value=string(total))
}

window {
    depth(0) {
        main()
    }
}
-- out/go/bubbletea/model.go --
